.TH REFS\-FUSE 8 "August 2025" "refsprogs @VERSION@"
.SH NAME
refs\-fuse \- Mount an ReFS volume as a filesystem backed by a FUSE daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B refs\-fuse
\fIdevice\fR \fImountpoint\fR [\fIFUSE options...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B refs\-fuse
will mount an ReFS volume in the filesystem tree at \fImountpoint\fR for
read-only access.
.SH OPTIONS
.B refs\-fuse
accepts most of the options that can be supplied when mounting FUSE filesystems.
.br
Examples (see \fBmount.fuse(8)\fR for more):
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Foreground mode. Suppresses daemonizing the filesystem when the mount is
successful.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
Single-threaded mode. Runs the FUSE file system operations in a single thread
(mainly useful for troubleshooting and debugging).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR MOUNT OPTIONS...
A comma-separated list of options to modify the mount. For more details, see the
.B MOUNT OPTIONS
section.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
.B refs\-fuse
accepts most FUSE mount options as described in the
.BR mount.fuse (8)
manpage, e.g. \fIdebug\fR for enabling debug output, \fInonempty\fR to allow
mounting over a non-empty directory, ...
.sp
.B refs\-fuse
supplies a few FUSE mount options by default, to bring it more in line with how
users generally expect a mounted filesystem to behave:
.RS
.sp
\fBro\fR to set the read-only flag for the mount. This makes FUSE return more
sensible defaults when attempting to write to the volume, which is not supported
at this time.
.sp
.br
\fBallow_other\fR to allow all users access to the volume.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option \fBnoallow_other\fR.
.sp
.br
\fBdefault_permissions\fR to enforce the permissions specified in the mount
options.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option
\fBnodefault_permissions\fR.
.sp
.br
\fBsubtype=refs\fR to add a specific subtype to the filesystem type reported by
e.g.
.BR mount (8)
\fB(Linux only)\fR.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option \fBnosubtype\fR or by
specifying another subtype.
.sp
.br
\fBblkdev\fR if we are mounting a device to make sure that FUSE uses the
\fBfuseblk\fR filesystem type (which is more optimal for a device backed
filesystem) \fB(Linux only)\fR.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option \fBnoblkdev\fR.
.sp
.br
\fBblksize=BLOCKSIZE\fR if we are mounting a device to make sure that FUSE uses
an appropriate block size for the \fBfuseblk\fR filesystem type \fB(Linux
only)\fR.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option \fBnoblksize\fR or by
supplying another block size.
.sp
.br
\fBfsname=NAME\fR to supply the name of the device that is being mounted to
FUSE so that it's reported correctly in mount listings.
.br
This default option can be negated with the custom option \fBnofsname\fR or by
supplying another name.
.sp
.RE
.sp
In addition to the standard FUSE mount options, \fBrefs\-fuse\fR supports the
following custom mount options:
.TP
.BI uid=NUMBER
Set the user ID (uid) of all files in the mounted volume to \fBNUMBER\fR.
.TP
.BI gid=NUMBER
Set the group ID (gid) of all files in the mounted volume to \fBNUMBER\fR.
.TP
.BI drive_[a-z]=PATH
Redirect any of the Windows drive letters (A: to Z:) to the path \fBPATH\fR in
the filesystem.
.br
This allows retargeting symbolic links and junction points to appropriate
locations in the local directory hierarchy, e.g. if the volume mapped to the
Windows drive 'D:' is mounted at "/mnt/storage" you can supply the mount
option 'drive_d=/mnt/storage' to make sure that cross-volume symlinks remain
valid and point to the appropriate local mount point.
.TP
.BI xattr_mode=MODE
Set the extended attributes mode of the volume to \fBMODE\fR.
.br
Valid modes are:
.sp
\fBnone\fR - No filesystem data is exposed as extended attributes.
.sp
.br
\fBstreams\fR - Named streams (alternate data streams, ADS) are exposed as
extended attributes \fB(default)\fR.
.sp
.br
\fBeas\fR - EAs (simple name-value pair attributes) are exposed as extended
attributes.
.br
EAs are mostly used for internal operating system information in modern contexts
and are typically not exposed to the user in Windows.
.sp
.br
\fBboth\fR - Named streams and EAs are both exposed as extended attributes.
.br
This can in theory lead to name collisions as two separate namespaces are
exposed as one.
.br
In practice the differing naming convention of EAs and named streams means
that they rarely clash.
.sp
.SH BUGS
.BR refs\-fuse
is currently limited to read-only operation, which isn't technically a bug but
a design limitation for now.

If you find a bug, then please report it to the refsprogs bug tracker on github:
https://github.com/unsound/refsprogs/issues
.hy
.SH AUTHORS
.B refs\-fuse
was written by Erik Larsson
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B refs\-fuse
is part of the
.B refsprogs
package.
.hy
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount.fuse (8)
